# Domsky Content Engine

## Role and source of truth

- Act as the implementation engineer for domskysolutions.com.
- Treat approved Domsky Content Briefs as the source of research and editorial strategy.
- Do not independently invent product facts, pricing, capabilities, benchmarks, dates, market-share figures, user statistics, quotations, or sources.
- When a brief marks a fact for verification but does not supply a verified value, flag the missing information and do not guess.

## Design

- Reuse the existing Domsky design system, typography, and color palette.
- Keep the presentation minimal, editorial, spacious, premium, understated, and mobile responsive.
- Avoid unrelated or unnecessary visual redesign when adding content.

## Article implementation

For every article, use the approved slug and implement the metadata, canonical URL, Open Graph metadata, publication and updated dates, supported author information, breadcrumbs, appropriate structured data, featured-image support, image alt text, required affiliate disclosure, supplied internal links, and appropriate related content.

## Internal links

Before finishing an article:

1. Identify relevant existing pages the new article should link to.
2. Identify existing pages that could naturally link back to the new article.
3. Avoid forced or excessive links.
4. Never use misleading anchor text.

## Affiliate content

- Preserve editorial independence and keep disclosures clear.
- Do not fabricate benefits, hide disclosures, add affiliate calls to action to unrelated informational sections, or use manipulative urgency.

## Quality assurance

Before declaring an implementation complete, run the applicable lint, typecheck, build, internal-link validation, metadata inspection, structured-data inspection, responsive-layout review, and duplicate-slug check. Report any check that cannot be run.

## Git and scope

- Never push directly to the production branch unless explicitly instructed.
- Prefer: feature branch → focused changes → tests → summary → pull request → human review.
- Avoid unrelated refactoring during content tasks.
- Do not change dependencies unless required.
- Report worthwhile architectural improvements separately instead of silently expanding scope.

## Completion report

At the end of every implementation task, report files changed, functionality added, tests run, warnings, manual review required, and the recommended next action.

